A concrete generic instance substitutes the generic parameters with concrete type arguments. The resulting type is ordinary: it has fields, constructors and the same access rules as any other type.

### Field and payload substitution

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Box type A = |
        value A,
    |

    int_box Box of I32 = Box(42)
    string_box Box of String = Box("hello")
]

`Box of I32` and `Box of String` are different semantic types. The field `value` has type `I32` in the first and `String` in the second.

### Generic choices

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Maybe type A ::
        Some | value A |,
        Empty,
    ;

    name Maybe of String = Maybe::Some("Priya")
    missing Maybe of String = Maybe::Empty
]

Choice payload matching uses the substituted payload types.

### Instance identity

Same constructor plus same concrete arguments yields the same instance type. Different arguments yield incompatible instance types.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Box type A = |
        value A,
    |

    a Box of I32 = Box(1)
    b Box of I32 = Box(2)
    -- c Box of String = a -- would be an error
]

### Constructor inference

Generic struct and choice constructors infer type arguments from immediate arguments and the immediate receiving type. A constructor may preserve an existing numeric origin through its known generic field shape until body-wide numeric constraints resolve it. This does not infer other type arguments or structure from later uses. No explicit constructor type arguments are supported.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Box type A = |
        value A,
    |

    -- Inferred from the constructor argument.
    value = Box(42)

    -- Inferred from the receiving type.
    text_box Box of String = Box("hello")
]

### Aligned receiver methods

A receiver method may be declared on a generic type when the method's generic parameters align exactly with the receiver constructor's parameters.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    Box type A = |
        value A,
    |

    get type A |this Box of A| -> A:
        return this.value
    ;

    box Box of I32 = Box(42)
    value = box.get()
]

Receiver methods on one concrete generic instance remain rejected.

### Generic bounds and evidence

Concrete instantiation with trait bounds requires visible reusable evidence. See @../traits/#generic-trait-bounds (Generic trait bounds).

### Deferred and rejected surfaces

- Recursive generic nominal types are rejected.
- Receiver methods on concrete instances are rejected.
- Generic external package functions and types are deferred.

Implicit failure follows the closed module boundary: an exported generic instance without an error slot must discharge it. See @../errors (Errors) for failure delivery.

### Concepts related to generic instances

- For type application see @#type-application (Type application).
- For trait bounds see @../traits/#generic-trait-bounds (Generic trait bounds).
